window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][16]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M16-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M16-L1 – Transduction Principles and Sensor Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Bachelor: Sensor Systems and Measurement Signal Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Select resistive, capacitive and\noptical transducers according to their characteristic curves,\nsensitivity and interfering quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the Measurand to the Electrical Signal<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor converts a physical quantity into an electrical quantity\nthat can be evaluated. The measurand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not identical to the reported numerical value. Physical coupling,\ntransduction, readout circuitry, sampling, calibration and processing\nlie between them. A strain transducer, for example, initially provides a\nchange in resistance rather than a digital result in units of length. A\nmeasurement chain must describe both the influence of the desired\nquantity and that of interfering quantities.<\/p>\n<p>A static sensor model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(x,z)+e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an interfering quantity (e.g. temperature) and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an error term. Around an operating point, the following approximation\nholds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y\\approx S_x\\Delta x+S_z\\Delta z,\\qquad S_x=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Sensitivity is the local slope, with its unit; resolution is the\nsmallest distinguishable change under specified conditions. Neither\nalone describes the systematic measurement error. A deviation on the\nreturn path of the characteristic curve is called hysteresis; drift\ndescribes changes over time while the measurand remains constant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_057_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Characteristic curves of a strain gauge, parallel-plate capacitor and photodiode, and a normalised first-order step response.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The four model curves distinguish static sensor\ntransduction characteristics from a dynamic step response; sensitivity\nand temporal response are separate properties.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three Transduction Principles<\/h2>\n<p>A metallic strain gauge changes its resistance as a result of\nmechanical deformation. For small strain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=\\Delta l/l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R/R_0=k\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds, with dimensionless gauge factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA strain gauge with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>350<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0=350\\ \\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\ \\mu\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{m}=10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces a change of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.7\\ \\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe resistance is considerably larger than the change of interest; a\nbridge is therefore used in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L2<\/a>.\nTemperature can also affect both resistance and mechanical strain.<\/p>\n<p>An ideal parallel-plate capacitor with area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand separation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\epsilon_0\\epsilon_r A/d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an air-gap measurement with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=100\\ \\mathrm{mm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmm, the capacitance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.77<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\approx1.77<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npF. If the separation decreases by 10%, the capacitance increases by\n11.1%. The sensitivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\epsilon_0\\epsilon_r A/d^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvaries with separation. Stray capacitance from wiring, humidity and\nfringing fields are significant at such small capacitances. A readout\ncircuit can convert capacitance into charging time, frequency or\nvoltage.<\/p>\n<p>At a given wavelength and within its linear range, a photodiode\napproximately delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{ph}=\\mathcal R_\\lambda P_{opt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R_\\lambda=0.5\\ \\mathrm{A/W}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\ \\mu\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe photocurrent is 5 µA. An inverting transimpedance amplifier with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_f=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkΩ ideally produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV relative to the reference potential for the chosen current direction.\nDark current, ambient light and wavelength affect the result; feedback\ncapacitance and diode capacitance influence bandwidth and stability.\nPhotocurrent does not constitute a distance measurement without a\ngeometrical and optical model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic Characteristic<\/h2>\n<p>Even with perfect static calibration, a time-varying measurand may\nappear delayed. A sensor with thermal inertia can initially be modelled\nas a first-order lag:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot y+y=Sx,\\qquad H(s)=\\frac{S}{1+s\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Following a step from equilibrium, the normalised response reaches\n63.2% of its final value after one time constant and 95% after\napproximately three time constants. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns, the cutoff frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2\\pi\\tau)=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz. A higher digital sampling frequency cannot remove this physical lag.\nThe idealised equation is not valid for every sensor; mechanical\nresonances may require higher-order models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selecting a Sensor for a Measurement Task<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task<\/th>\n<th>Candidate<\/th>\n<th>Main limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Local strain in a beam<\/td>\n<td>Strain gauge<\/td>\n<td>Bonding, temperature, load path<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small displacement without mechanical contact<\/td>\n<td>Capacitive displacement sensor<\/td>\n<td>Stray fields, contamination, geometry<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optical power or interruption of a light beam<\/td>\n<td>Photodiode<\/td>\n<td>Ambient light, spectrum, saturation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For the subsequent model chain, we select local tensile strain from 0\nto 2000 µm/m in an idealised uniformly strained component. We use the\n350 Ω strain gauge with gauge factor 2. A force value would only be\njustified with a mechanical model (geometry, elastic modulus, load\npath). The digital result is therefore expressed in µm/m. The later\nobjective is to distinguish static measurement error from dynamic lag at\na sampling rate of 100 Hz, not to demonstrate the performance of a real\nmeasuring instrument.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 350 Ω strain gauge with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis strained by 500 µm/m. What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>350<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.35<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">350\\cdot2\\cdot500\\cdot10^{-6}=0.35\\ \\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The separation of an ideal parallel-plate capacitor doubles. What\nhappens if the area remains unchanged?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Capacitance halves; the magnitude of the\nlocal sensitivity falls to one quarter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nA/W and 20 µW of optical power, what ideal photocurrent results?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 8 µA; dark current and amplifier errors are\nnot included.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A sensor takes 0.6 s to reach approximately 95% of the step.\nEstimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Approximately 0.2 s for a first-order model.\nA faster ADC does not change this time constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.tudelft.nl/course-lectures/sensors/\">TU Delft\nOpenCourseWare – Measurement Science: Sensors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Week 2, Sensors:\noverview of resistive, capacitive and inductive transducers; video not\nreviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ni.com/en/shop/data-acquisition/sensor-fundamentals/measuring-strain-with-strain-gages.html\">NI\n– Measuring Strain with Strain Gages<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Signal Conditioning for\nStrain Gages: bridge excitation, offset, amplification and filtering. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M16-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M16-L2 – Bridge Circuit, Amplification and Interference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Bachelor: Sensor Systems and Measurement Signal Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine bridge voltage, gain,\noffset and signal swing margins in an analogue measurement chain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differential Measurement with the Wheatstone Bridge<\/h2>\n<p>Four resistors form two voltage dividers with a common excitation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_E=3.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. On the left, the upper resistor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the lower one is the active strain gauge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0(1+\\delta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non the right, both resistors are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the ideal model, the amplifier inputs do not load the bridge. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=k\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_L=U_E\\frac{1+\\delta}{2+\\delta},\\qquad U_R=\\frac{U_E}{2},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mfrac><mi>δ<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d=U_L-U_R=U_E\\frac{\\delta}{2(2+\\delta)}\\approx\\frac{U_E}{4}k\\varepsilon.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The approximation requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≪<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\delta|\\ll1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt 2000 µm/m and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe exact equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.2934<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.2934<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV, while the linear approximation gives 3.3000 mV. The bridge\napproximation error is about 0.2% of the signal here. The sign\ndeliberately depends on the bridge arm and input assignment.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_058_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Quarter bridge with an active lower-left resistor and differential amplifier; nominal output characteristic and permissible model window.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The strain-gauge quarter bridge converts a small\nresistance change into a differential voltage for amplification; the\nlinear operating range applies only within the model\nwindow.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gain and Signal Swing<\/h2>\n<p>An instrumentation amplifier ideally produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_A=U_{ref}+G U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{ref}=1.65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, the output at maximum tensile strain is 2.309 V. The nominal output\nsensitivity is 0.330 mV per µm/m. The small change in bridge voltage can\nthus be used effectively without approaching the 3.3 V ADC limit. The\nreference input shifts the output level but is not automatically an\nerror-free voltage source. The experiment nominally operates between\nzero and 2000 µm/m. Negative strain produces a signal below the\nreference point and must be approved separately if needed.<\/p>\n<p>Real amplifiers do not necessarily permit the entire range between\nthe supply rails. For the teaching model, we assume a permissible output\nwindow of 0.15 to 3.15 V, a maximum total output offset of 50 mV and a\nmaximum positive gain error of 3%. In the worst-case upper load\ncondition,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.65<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>206<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.0032934<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.3784<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.65+0.05+206\\cdot0.0032934=2.3784<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV; the margin to 3.15 V is about 0.772 V. At the lower end, at least\n1.60 V remains. The common-mode condition must also be checked:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{cm}=(U_L+U_R)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlies between 1.650 and 1.652 V. For an actual device, the permissible\ninput common-mode voltage may depend on gain and output voltage. A valid\noutput level alone therefore does not demonstrate circuit linearity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interference Coupling and Noise Budget<\/h2>\n<p>Common-mode rejection ratio (CMRR) is the ratio of differential gain\nto common-mode gain. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{CMRR}_{dB}=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na change of 0.1 V at the common-mode input ideally corresponds to an\ninput-referred differential voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1/10^4=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµV and 2 mV after amplification. This corresponds to about 6.1 µm/m. The\nvalue applies only at the specified frequency and operating conditions,\nsince CMRR often decreases with increasing frequency. Wiring impedances\nand asymmetric filtering can introduce further common-mode to\ndifferential-mode conversion. A twisted differential pair, suitable\nshielding, symmetrical filtering and short connections can reduce\ninterference. A digital averaging filter is no substitute for analogue\ninterference suppression before the ADC.<\/p>\n<p>An input offset of 50 µV would ideally shift the output by 10 mV. In\nstrain units, this corresponds to roughly 30.3 µm/m. Zero-point\ncalibration can capture a constant offset but not automatically its\ntemperature drift. For uncorrelated noise contributions with standard\ndeviations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe relation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{ges}=\\sqrt{\\sum_i\\sigma_i^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(subscript ges denotes total). Periodic interference, correlated drift\nand saturation must not be included in the budget in the same way\nwithout verification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy and Excitation<\/h2>\n<p>In the unloaded bridge case,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>700<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.71<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3/700=4.71<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmA flows through each branch. The active 350 Ω resistor dissipates\napproximately 7.78 mW as heat; the entire bridge requires approximately\n31.1 mW. Self-heating can shift the measured value. Halving the\nexcitation voltage halves the sensitivity and reduces power dissipation\nto one quarter; in general, dissipation varies quadratically with\nvoltage. Ratiometric operation uses the same excitation as the ADC\nreference: a common scaling of bridge voltage and ADC reference can\ncancel out in the code, provided that the gain, output reference and\nother error sources are compatible. The subsequent example uses a\nconstant common 3.3 V reference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the linear bridge voltage at 500 µm/m.<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.825<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3\\cdot2\\cdot500\\cdot10^{-6}/4=0.825<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 2000 µm/m, is the ideal output within the model window?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.65<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.0032934<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.9674<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.65+400\\cdot0.0032934=2.9674<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV. Yes, but only about 183 mV of margin remains before additional\nerrors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the input-referred differential interference at 60 dB CMRR\nand a 50 mV common-mode change?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 50 µV, corresponding to 10 mV at the output\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The bridge excitation is reduced to 1.65 V. How does the nominal\npower dissipation change?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> It falls to one quarter (7.78 mW for the\nentire bridge); the bridge sensitivity halves.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.ni.com/en/shop/data-acquisition/sensor-fundamentals/measuring-strain-with-strain-gages.html\">NI\n– Measuring Strain with Strain Gages<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Signal Conditioning for\nStrain Gages: bridge excitation, offset, amplification and filtering. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/analog-dialogue/articles/how-to-stay-out-of-deep-water.html\">Analog\nDevices – How to Stay Out of Deep Water when Designing with Bridge\nSensors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Introduction: bridge differential signal, common-mode\nvoltage and offset. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M16-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M16-L3 – Sampling, Quantisation and Converter Errors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Bachelor: Sensor Systems and Measurement Signal Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine ADC resolution and the\nerror budget, and calculate alias frequencies and requirements for the\nanalogue prefilter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sampling and Frequency Ambiguity<\/h2>\n<p>An ADC captures a continuous-time signal at discrete instants\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_n=nT_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s=1/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor band-limited signals, ideal reconstruction from uniformly spaced\nsamples is possible if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s&gt;2f_{max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mathematical model also assumes an ideal reconstruction filter and\nno quantisation. In practice, an analogue anti-aliasing filter must\nsufficiently suppress unwanted spectral components before sampling. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz, the Nyquist frequency is 50 Hz; interference at 70 Hz appears at 30\nHz because<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(2\\pi\\cdot70 n/100)=\\cos(2\\pi\\cdot30 n/100).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>After digitisation, these two cosine signals cannot be distinguished\nfrom their samples alone. The error cannot be eliminated by increasing\nthe digital bit depth. For real-valued signals, the folded frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a=|f-mf_s|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis selected with a suitable integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nso that it lies between 0 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe phase relationship depends, among other things, on the sign before\nthe absolute value is taken.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_059_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"70 Hz and 30 Hz cosines with common sample values; idealised quantisation characteristic with midpoint reconstruction.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Two continuous cosine signals yield identical\nsamples at the selected instants; subsequent quantization represents\nonly discrete amplitude levels.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>ADC Characteristic and Quantisation Noise<\/h2>\n<p>As a continuing teaching model, we use a unipolar ideal 12-bit ADC\nwith a 3.3 V full-scale range,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4096<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{12}=4096<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncodes and quantisation step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4096<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80566<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=3.3/4096=0.80566<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le U&lt;3.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, it produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\lfloor U/q\\rfloor<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nabove and below the measurement range, the code is limited. The\nreconstructed voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat U=(c+1/2)q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout overload,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat U-U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlies between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-q/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(boundary convention). With this definition, the range boundaries do not\ncoincide with the reconstructed voltages of the outermost codes.<\/p>\n<p>Relative to the nominal sensitivity of 0.330 mV/(µm/m), one LSB\ncorresponds to approximately 2.44 µm/m. If the quantisation error is\nmodelled as uniformly distributed and independent of the input, then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>q<\/mi><msqrt><mn>12<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.233<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_q=\\frac{q}{\\sqrt{12}}=0.233\\ \\mathrm{mV}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This approximation is useful when the input varies sufficiently or\nwhen dither is present, but not for every constant signal: without\nnoise, the error can remain the same across all samples. For an ideal\nfull-scale sine wave, the RMS signal value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{FS}/(2\\sqrt2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand quantisation noise yield the familiar relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.02<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.76<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SNR}_{dB}\\approx6.02N+1.76<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor twelve bits, this is about 74 dB under precisely these model\nassumptions. A smaller input signal makes less use of the dynamic range.\nENOB (effective number of bits) is determined from SINAD in a sinusoidal\ntest, thus including distortion, and must not be confused with the\nnominal resolution alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Additional Sources of Error<\/h2>\n<p>An ADC can exhibit offset error, gain error, differential\nnonlinearity (unequal code widths) and integral nonlinearity (deviation\nof the characteristic curve). A linear calibration can fully compensate\nonly for affine deviations. The reference voltage affects scaling; input\nimpedance and the charging time of a sample-and-hold circuit can cause\nadditional settling errors. With small timing jitter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe voltage deviation is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>U<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot U(t)\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSteep edges or high frequencies therefore increase the timing-related\nerror budget.<\/p>\n<p>In the later experiment, we also assume independent analogue output\nnoise with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_A=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. The root-sum-square budget gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0.233<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.014<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2^2+0.233^2}=2.014<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV at the ADC, or approximately 6.10 µm/m relative to the nominal\nsensitivity. Here, analogue noise rather than quantisation dominates.\nMore nominal bits alone therefore make little difference to the actual\naccuracy. The calculation assumes that no clipping occurs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Designing the Anti-Aliasing Path<\/h2>\n<p>For a desired signal up to 5 Hz and interference at 70 Hz, we\nconsider two buffered identical RC low-pass filters, each with a 10 Hz\ncutoff frequency. The overall amplitude response is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H(f)|=1/(1+(f/10)^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt 5 Hz, 80% of the amplitude remains (approximately −1.94 dB); at 70\nHz, 2% remains (approximately −34 dB). Thus, 100 mV of interference\nbecomes 2 mV at 70 Hz, which can appear at 30 Hz after sampling. The\namplitude of the desired 5 Hz signal is also measurably changed. A\ndifferent filter or a higher sampling rate may be required if these\ndeviations cannot be tolerated. The filter is a design example and is\nnot included in the simplified dynamic experiment in M16-L5. A real\nspecification encompasses all relevant interference bands, phase\nresponse and settling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What alias results from a 123 Hz cosine at a sampling rate of 100\nHz?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 23 Hz.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat 16 bits and a 3.3 V full-scale range?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 50.35 µV; the analogue 2 mV noise remains\nunchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which code results at exactly 1.65 V in the ideal 12-bit\nmodel?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 2048, with a reconstructed value of\napproximately 1.650403 V.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Analogue interference is 0.2 V at 70 Hz. What amplitude reaches the\nADC after the two 10 Hz RC stages?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 4 mV; it can appear in the digital signal as\na 30 Hz component.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/media/en/training-seminars/tutorials/MT-001.pdf\">Analog\nDevices / Walt Kester – MT-001, Quantization Noise and SNR<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rev. A,\np. 1, Quantization Noise Model: ideal ADC and quantization error;\nassumptions of the SNR model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.tudelft.nl/course-lectures/sensors/\">TU Delft\nOpenCourseWare – Measurement Science: Sensors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Week 2, Sensors:\ntransduction and sensor types; contextual reading only, not evidence for\nADC quantization. See MT-001 and MT-002 for the latter. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/media/en/training-seminars/tutorials/MT-002.pdf\">Analog\nDevices / Walt Kester – MT-002, What the Nyquist Criterion Means to Your\nSampled Data System Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rev. A, p. 1, Fig. 1: sampling, analog\nprefilter and digital signal processing. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M16-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M16-L4 – Filtering and Estimation with Noisy Data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Bachelor: Sensor Systems and Measurement Signal Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design a causal filter by\nconsidering noise suppression, frequency response and time delay\ntogether.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random Fluctuations and Systematic Error<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor provides multiple observations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=s[n]+e[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the true signal is constant within a measurement window and the\nadditive errors are independent, have zero mean and have variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe arithmetic mean is an unbiased estimator of the constant signal. Its\nvariance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHowever, a constant offset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains unchanged. A noisy signal with little mean error may allow more\naccurate evaluation than a smooth but incorrectly calibrated signal.<\/p>\n<p>For correlated errors with autocovariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma[k]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe more general expression is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Var}(\\bar e)=\\frac{1}{N^2}\\left(N\\gamma[0]+2\\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}(N-k)\\gamma[k]\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Slowly varying errors or periodic interference therefore do not\nnecessarily follow the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrule for the standard deviation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Moving Average as an FIR Filter<\/h2>\n<p>A causal moving average over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsamples is given by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=\\frac1N\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}x[n-k].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It has a finite impulse response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[k]=1/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le k&lt;N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand zero otherwise. In steady operation it can be calculated efficiently\nusing the running sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s[n]=s[n-1]+x[n]-x[n-N]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis requires storing the oldest value and the sum. With integer\narithmetic, the accumulator must accommodate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes the maximum code; for 15 values from a 12-bit ADC this is 61425,\nwhich fits in an unsigned 16-bit integer without further signed or\noffset arithmetic. Initial values before measurements begin must be\ntreated as assumptions; here we only output a valid filter result after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nactual measurements.<\/p>\n<p>The transfer function for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi f/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows from the geometric series:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(e^{j\\omega})=e^{-j\\omega(N-1)/2}\\frac{\\sin(N\\omega/2)}{N\\sin(\\omega/2)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe limiting value is one. At 100 Hz and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe first zero occurs at 20 Hz; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit occurs at 6.67 Hz. For both filters the group delay is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(N-1)/(2f_s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naway from zeros and phase discontinuities, i.e. 20 and 70 ms\nrespectively. This refers to the filter delay, excluding additional\ncomputation time or communication delay. An ideal positive step is fully\nreflected in the output after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnew values; the transition starts immediately with the first new value,\nand for odd\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe halfway point is reached after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(N-1)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nintervals.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_060_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Frequency response and normalised step response of causal moving averages with 5 and 15 samples at 100 Hz.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A longer moving-average window suppresses higher\nfrequencies more strongly but also delays the step response; both\neffects need to be assessed together.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursive Low-Pass Filter<\/h2>\n<p>A memory-efficient alternative is the exponential moving average\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=\\alpha x[n]+(1-\\alpha)y[n-1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\alpha\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe filter has a pole at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=1-\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an infinitely long, decaying impulse response. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=1-e^{-T_s/\\tau}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe decay rate corresponds to that of an ideal first-order low-pass\nfilter with time constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe recursive filter does not have linear phase. For a constant signal\nand independent white noise, the steady-state output has variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2\\alpha/(2-\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe initial value influences the result during settling. When\nmeasurements are missing, a decision must be made as to whether the time\ninterval should be adjusted, the state retained or the output marked as\ninvalid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Filter Selection for the Measurement Chain<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=6.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµm/m and 100 Hz, the ideal expected standard deviations are 2.73 µm/m\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 1.58 µm/m for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the halfway point of a step must be detected within 30 ms,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nappears plausible;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexceeds the budget even in the ideal filter model. Conversely, a longer\nwindow may be useful for slow static measurements. The case study in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L5<\/a>\ncalculates both the actual noise in a dataset and the detection time. A\ncomparison requires identical raw data and a predefined metric. For\nfilter selection in a control system, the phase margin and total loop\nlatency must also be considered.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the expected standard deviation for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµm/m and 9 independent measurements?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 4 µm/m. A constant offset remains.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What group delay results for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 200 Hz?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 25 ms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the output of a filter with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the first new value of a step from 500 to 1500 µm/m?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1500<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>700<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4\\cdot500+1500)/5=700<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµm/m; at the third new value, 1100 µm/m.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms, determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1813<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-e^{-0.2}\\approx0.1813<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe initial value must be documented.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dspguide.com/ch15.htm\">Steven W. Smith – The\nScientist and Engineer’s Guide to DSP, Chapter 15<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 15,\nintroduction: moving average and limits of frequency selectivity. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/media/en/training-seminars/tutorials/MT-002.pdf\">Analog\nDevices / Walt Kester – MT-002, What the Nyquist Criterion Means to Your\nSampled Data System Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Rev. A, p. 1, Fig. 1: sampling, analog\nprefilter and digital signal processing. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M16-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M16-L5 – Case Study: Calibration and Quality Verification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Bachelor: Sensor Systems and Measurement Signal Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calibrate a complete synthetic\nmeasurement chain and assess it using independent verification points,\nnoise and step-response delay.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model and Test Plan<\/h2>\n<p>The measurement chain is intended to measure tensile strain between 0\nand 2000 µm/m. The nominal characteristic and quantisation come from the\npreceding lessons. The simulated actual forward characteristic\nincorporates the exact bridge, a 2.5% gain deviation, a 35 mV output\noffset and independent, normally distributed output noise with a\nstandard deviation of 2 mV. The ideal ADC has twelve bits and a 3.3 V\nfull-scale range. The simulated reference strain is exact; for a later,\nmore realistic uncertainty budget we separately assume an independent\nstandard uncertainty of 2 µm/m for the reference. The data are entirely\nsynthetic and do not constitute measurements.<\/p>\n<p>Calibration uses twelve independent values at each of 0, 500, 1000,\n1500 and 2000 µm/m. A separate static verification uses 40 values at\neach of 250, 750, 1250 and 1750 µm/m. We then also test a 15 mV change\nin output offset at the same strain levels. Under nominal calibration\nconditions, the model should achieve an RMSE of no more than 8 µm/m and\na maximum observed absolute deviation of no more than 25 µm/m across\nthese verification points. For an ideal step at 100 Hz, a filter should\nreduce the plateau standard deviation below 3 µm/m and cross the halfway\npoint of the step within 30 ms. These are teaching criteria, not a\nstandard or statistically established limits. The step experiment\ndeliberately excludes mechanical inertia and analogue filtering so that\nthe digital contribution is apparent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Estimation and Inverse Characteristic<\/h2>\n<p>Given a known reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an observed voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe estimate the forward model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=a+bx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby least squares. We minimise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i(y_i-a-bx_i)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nyielding<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat b=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)(y_i-\\bar y)}{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2},\\qquad\\hat a=\\bar y-\\hat b\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a new measured voltage, the inverse estimate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=(y-\\hat a)/\\hat b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe method requires a sufficiently accurate reference and an adequate\nlinear model over this range. Significant reference errors or pronounced\nnonlinearity would require other models or uncertainty methods.\nVerification data are kept separate from estimation: a small training\nresidual alone does not demonstrate that a new measurement will be\nreliable.<\/p>\n<p>The example calculation is reproducible with Python and NumPy; the\norder of random values is determined by a fixed seed. In addition to\nevaluating the independent cases, the code checks residual conditions,\nthe absence of clipping, moving average filters and the aliasing\nexample. <code>np.polyfit<\/code> returns the slope before the\nintercept.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">1601<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">3.3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4096<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> voltage(e, drift<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> np.asarray(e) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    bridge <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">3.3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> d <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> d))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.65<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.035<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">200<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.025<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> bridge <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> drift<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> sample(e, drift<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> voltage(e, drift) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> rng.normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.002<\/span>, np.shape(e))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    code <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.clip(np.floor(v <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> q), <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4095<\/span>).astype(<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>((v <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span> (v <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">3.3<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> (code <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> q<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.repeat(np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2001<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sample(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b, a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.polyfit(x, y, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> y <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> (a <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(r.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(r<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>x) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-7<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>levels <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">250<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2000<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.repeat(levels, <span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sample(z)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v_drift <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sample(z, <span class=\"fl\">.015<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>nominal <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.65<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.00033<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cal <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cal_drift <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (v_drift<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> stats(estimate):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> estimate<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.mean(e), np.sqrt(np.mean(e<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)), np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(e))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;fit&#39;<\/span>, a, b, <span class=\"st\">&#39;sV&#39;<\/span>, np.sqrt(r<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name, est <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;nominal&#39;<\/span>,nominal),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;cal&#39;<\/span>,cal),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;drift&#39;<\/span>,cal_drift)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(name,stats(est))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> level <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> levels:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    errors<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(cal<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z)[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>level]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(level, errors.mean(), errors.std(ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># independent verification data: pooled scatter and observed max mean error<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(np.var((cal<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z)[z<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>k],ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">39<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> levels)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">156<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;pooled&#39;<\/span>,s,<span class=\"st\">&#39;u&#39;<\/span>,np.hypot(s,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># dynamic trial: input step at sample 100, 100 Hz<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">300<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> truth<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.where(n<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">500<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1500<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>raw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(sample(truth)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>filters<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> N <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    out<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.full(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(raw),np.nan)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> out[N<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.convolve(raw,np.ones(N)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N,<span class=\"st\">&#39;valid&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-48\"><a href=\"#cb1-48\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    filters[N]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>out<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-49\"><a href=\"#cb1-49\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    cross<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.flatnonzero((n<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span>(out<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>))[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-50\"><a href=\"#cb1-50\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(N, np.std(out[<span class=\"dv\">130<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">280<\/span>],ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>), cross<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-51\"><a href=\"#cb1-51\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(out[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.mean(raw[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>N:])) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-52\"><a href=\"#cb1-52\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(np.cos(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">70<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>), np.cos(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Static Quality Verification and Drift<\/h2>\n<p>The regression yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6843213<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat a=1.6843213<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0003381104<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat b=0.0003381104<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV/(µm/m) and a residual standard deviation of 2.130 mV (58 degrees of\nfreedom). The exact sensor bridge is slightly nonlinear; the straight\nline approximates it over the specified range. The independent\nverification results are:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method / condition<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Mean error (µm/m)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">RMSE (µm/m)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Largest absolute error (µm/m)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal characteristic, without calibration<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">129.30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">130.13<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">157.96<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calibration line<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.70<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16.64<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Original calibration line after +15 mV offset drift<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">46.35<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">46.81<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">65.49<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The calibrated chain meets the nominal static model criteria. The\nunchanged calibration line fails after offset drift: even without noise,\nan additional bias of approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.015<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>44.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.015/\\hat b=44.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµm/m is expected. A new zero-point measurement could help if the error\nis purely an offset; if slope or hysteresis also change, this is\ninsufficient.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Verification strain (µm/m)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Mean deviation (µm/m)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Standard deviation (µm/m)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">250<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.75<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">750<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.97<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.19<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1250<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.64<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.94<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1750<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.28<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.89<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The pooled within-level standard deviation is 5.98 µm/m. For a single\nnew stationary observation, combining this in quadrature with the\nassumed independent reference uncertainty gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>5.98<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.31<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{teil}=\\sqrt{5.98^2+2^2}=6.31<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµm/m (the subscript teil denotes partial). This is explicitly only a\npartial budget: regression parameters, residual nonlinearity,\ntemperature, reference correlation, long-term stability and the actual\nmechanical reference must be considered separately in a complete\nuncertainty assessment. The maximum observed group mean error of 1.97\nµm/m is not a guaranteed error limit. Quantisation and the simulated\nanalogue noise are already included in the observed scatter; do not add\nthem a second time. Simply doubling the partial budget does not produce\na certified 95% interval.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_061_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Calibration characteristic, independent errors under normal and drift conditions, and step responses of the three windows.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Calibration data, independent check measurements and\nwindow step responses are shown separately; fitting calibration data\ndoes not replace drift testing.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic Test and Acceptance<\/h2>\n<p>The strain jumps from 500 to 1500 µm/m at 1.00 s (sample 100). The\nthree variants are derived from the same raw data. The standard\ndeviation is calculated over plateau samples 130 to 279. The\nhalfway-point time is that of the first sample from sample 100 onwards\nwith an estimated strain of at least 1000 µm/m.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Moving average window<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Plateau scatter (µm/m)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Threshold-crossing delay<\/th>\n<th>Model criterion<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.37<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0 ms<\/td>\n<td>Noise too high<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.70<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20 ms<\/td>\n<td>Met<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">15<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.27<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">70 ms<\/td>\n<td>Too slow<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>We select\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor this test plan. With a finite sample size, the measured noise\ndiffers from the ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpectation; consecutive moving averages are correlated. The time is\nrelative to the first new sample. For a real asynchronous step, up to\napproximately one sampling period is added, as well as sensor, analogue\nfilter, processing and transport time. The 30 ms requirement for a real\ncomplete chain is therefore not demonstrated. Real acceptance would\nrequire reference traceability, ascending and descending load series,\ntemperature tests, immunity to interference, mechanical coupling,\nclipping/error flags and documented dynamic reference measurements. In\nconjunction with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a>,\nthe filter state, measurement time and validity status must be preserved\nduring data transport.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A new measurement is 2.0 V. What is the calibrated strain?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.6843213<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.0003381104<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>934<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.0-1.6843213)/0.0003381104\\approx934<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµm/m; interpret only within the calibrated range.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are the reference levels 250, 750, 1250 and 1750 not used for\nfitting?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> They allow independent verification of\ninterpolation without the line being able to adapt to these errors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the moving average when there is a 15 mV offset\ndrift?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Averaging reduces random scatter; the\nconstant drift error remains approximately 44 µm/m.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which window is suitable for an ideal digital deadline of 30 ms and\nplateau scatter below 3 µm/m?<br />\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeets both criteria in this dataset; actual total latency and\nstatistical repeatability remain unresolved.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> M17 covers communication systems and\nthe transmission of digitised information.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section3/mpc365.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Data analysis and model validation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.3.6.5: calibration data\nand model validation. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section3/mpc366.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Calibration of future measurements<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §2.3.6.6: correction of\nfuture measurements using the inverse calibration curve. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section3/mpc3672.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Uncertainty for linear calibration using check standards<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\n§2.3.6.7.2: uncertainty from check standard measurements. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dspguide.com/ch15.htm\">Steven W. Smith – The\nScientist and Engineer’s Guide to DSP, Chapter 15<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 15,\nMoving Average Filters: further reading on moving averages and noise\nreduction; specific test data are synthetic. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
